WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:06.780
To analyze the statistical significance of
the identified peptide spectrum matrix.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.480
We will introduce the concept
of Spectral Dictionaries.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:16.490
Imagine that a PSM search
of 1,000 spectra from

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:21.660
a human sample against the human
proteome results in 100

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.800
peptide spectrum matches whose
score exceeds a threshold.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.910
What is a fraction of erroneous
peptide spectrum matches among

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.160
these 100 peptide spectrum matches?

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.640
I'll give you a hint of how it may
be possible to answer this question,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.400
and here's the hint.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:47.420
Let's repeat the same experiment for
a randomly generated

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:52.510
DecoyProteome of the same
size as the human proteome.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:56.490
Of course, we don't care about
peptide spectrum matches

00:00:56.490 --> 00:01:01.100
identified in the Decoy Proteome:
there are simply statistical artifacts.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:08.190
What we care about is the number
of such hits in the Decoy Proteome.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:15.090
For example, if you identify 5 peptide
spectrum matches in DecoyProteome,

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:20.630
then you expect that five over 100,
or 5% of

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:25.730
PSMs identified in the real
proteome are incorrect.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.680
And therefore, we define the notion of
"false discovery rate" as simply the ratio

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:39.460
of the number of peptide spectral
matches identified in DecoyProteome

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:45.700
over the number of peptides spectral
matches identified in the real proteome.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:51.070
And when we run this experiment
on the T-Rex spectra, then

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:58.760
we will identify 27 peptide spectrum
matches in the UniProt+ real database

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.520
and only 1 peptide spectrum
match in the DecoyProteome of

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.130
the same size for a threshold of 100.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:15.014
Which means that, in this experiment,
FDR will be a respectable 3.7%.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:22.590
But does it mean that we just found
approximately 27 T-Rex peptides?

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.820
Not quite, because many of the peptides
that we identify are simply

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.740
laboratory contaminants

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:30.840
that are present in every experiment.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.740
For example, keratin from human skin.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:39.540
There are currently millions of
tiny particles of my skin and

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:47.270
skin of the people who pass through this
room floating in the air in this room.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:53.570
The questions that we have to answer to
figure out which of the identified peptide

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:58.480
spectrum matches are correct
is how to estimate

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.950
the statistical significance of
individual peptide spectrum matches

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.880
rather than the bulk false discovery
rate for the entire sample?

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.660
To answer this question,
we will bring in a monkey.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.730
Give this monkey a typewriter and

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:21.020
let the monkey type random keys on this
typewriter for a very long time.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:29.330
Afterwards, let's check how many correctly
spelled English words the monkey generated.

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:34.030
We can use Webster dictionary to check it,
or whatever monkey dictionary to check it.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:36.770
In this particular case, the monkey

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:41.010
generated 13 correctly
spelled English words.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.830
Does it mean that the monkey can spell?

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:48.610
Well to answer this question
we probably need to evaluate

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:53.950
what is the expected number or
words from the dictionary

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:58.750
that appear in a randomly
generated text or in other words,

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:04.130
we need to solve the following:
The Monkey and the Typewriter Problem.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:06.960
Find the expected number of strings

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.120
from a dictionary appearing
in randomly generated text.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.130
But what does it have to do
with mass spectrometry?

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:20.920
Well, at the same time, we want to solve
the following mass spectrometry problem:

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:26.630
To find the expected number of high-scoring
peptides, which is to find the expected

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:32.340
number of high-scoring peptides against
a given spectrum in a decoy proteome.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:39.510
The input to this problem is a Spectrum,
an integer n, and a score threshold.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:44.460
And the output is the expected
of peptides in a decoy proteome

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.470
of length n that score a least
threshold against Spectrum.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:57.850
You may be wondering what is there
similarity or any relevance of the expected

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:02.380
number of the high-scoring peptide problem and
the monkey and the typewriter problem.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:07.090
It is not clear that these
problems are equivalent, but they are.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.310
To explain why this is
actually the same problem,

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:16.810
I will introduce the notion
of spectral dictionary.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:21.100
Dictionary of spectrum under
a given threshold is simply the set

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:26.940
of all peptides with a score of at
least threshold against Spectrum.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:33.720
There will be many of these peptides,
and as soon as the generate

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:38.120
the dictionary for
a given spectrum, we can reformulate

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:43.330
the expected number of high-scoring
peptides problem as the following problem.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:48.090
We want to find the expected
number of peptides from Dictionary

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:51.439
occurring in a decoy proteome of length n.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:56.500
And let's make one more step
to reformulate this problem.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.580
We've reformulated its output.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.880
Let's now reformulate its input.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.820
And the new input will be simply all
peptides from Dictionary of Spectrum and

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:07.470
an integer n.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:12.000
And output, the expected number
of strings from the dictionary

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.730
occurring in a decoy proteome of lens F.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:17.180
Take a look at this problem.

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:20.220
This is exactly the monkey and

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:24.065
the dictionary problem for
a specific set of peptides

00:06:24.065 --> 00:06:28.741
given by dictionary or
spectrum under a given threshold.

